<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Sine</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1891 CE (approx.) · capital Diakhao</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Serer kingdom of west-central Senegal, ruled by the Guelowar dynasty and long a stronghold of Serer religion against Muslim jihad.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> tradition holds that around 1350 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelowar">Guelowar</a>, a maternal lineage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> origin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a>, came to the Serer country and married into its old landholding families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maysa-wali">Maysa Wali</a>, remembered as the first Guelowar king, founded the line of rulers titled Maad a Sinig. The royal seat was later fixed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diakhao">Diakhao</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king ruled with a council of great chiefs and with the Lingeer, the queen mother or royal sister. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> religion, with its saltigue priests and rain rites, remained central, and Sine resisted conversion. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>. In 1867 its army defeated and killed the jihadist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maba-diakhou-ba">Maba Diakhou Ba</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fandane-thiouthioune">Fandane-Thiouthioune</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sine became a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and was absorbed into the colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a> in 1891. Kings continued as colonial chiefs; the last, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahecor-diouf">Mahecor Diouf</a>, died in 1969, after which independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> ended the monarchy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-sine.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Sine" width="320" height="218"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Sine_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Diakhao</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maysa Wali, Kumba Ndoffene Famak Diouf, Mahecor Diouf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serer religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Senegal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Siin, Sine</dd></dl></aside></div>

